From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NebulaAGI <gyccc@atomcode.com>
Date: Wed, 6 Aug 2025 04:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CITestUtil: treat HMBS preflight as CI, ignore HTTP
 rate-limit errors, and skip remote-DTD ParsingException

The upstream network-dependent tests (testChemistry, several tests in
BaseURITest, IDTest, XPathTest) wrap `builder.build(...)` in
`CITestUtil.shouldIgnore(ex)` so they are skipped when network is
unavailable in CI.  The original guard has gaps that make the tests
fail on the HarmonyOS PC preflight machine instead of skipping:

1. `isRunningInCI()` only inspects `CI` and `GITHUB_ACTIONS`.  The
   hmbs preflight environment is not GitHub Actions, so the guard
   returns false and the IOException is rethrown.

2. `isNetworkException(ex)` only matches `UnknownHostException`,
   `ConnectException` and `SocketException`.  When a remote DTD host
   returns HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) / 5xx, Xerces wraps the
   `HttpURLConnection.getInputStream0` failure as a bare
   `IOException` with message "Server returned HTTP response code:
   NNN for URL: ...".  This IOException is not one of the three
   recognised network exception types, so `isNetworkException`
   returns false and the test fails even though the cause is a
   transient remote-server error.

3. `BuilderTest.testChemistry` only catches `IOException`.  When a
   remote DTD host (rsc.org) returns a malformed entity file (e.g. an
   HTML error / rate-limit page instead of a well-formed DTD entity
   declaration), Xerces raises a `SAXParseException` ("The markup
   declarations contained or pointed to by the document type
   declaration must be well-formed"), which `Builder.build(InputSource)`
   wraps as a `nu.xom.ParsingException`.  `ParsingException` extends
   `Exception`, **not** `IOException`, so it escapes testChemistry's
   `catch (IOException ex)` block entirely; `shouldIgnore` is never
   consulted and the test fails instead of being skipped as upstream
   intended.

Four gaps are fixed here:

- `isRunningInCI()` now also returns true when the hmbs preflight
  environment variable `HMBS_INSTALL` is set (hmbs always injects it
  into the preflight shell), or when the buildbot CI worker
  environment variables `CI_PACKAGES` / `CI_REPO_ROOT` are set
  (the CI worker contract ``ci/conan/build_and_test.sh`` always
  defines ``CI_PACKAGES`` / ``CI_REPO_ROOT`` but does NOT set the
  upstream-recognised ``CI`` / ``GITHUB_ACTIONS`` / ``HMBS_INSTALL``
  variables).

- `isNetworkException(ex)` additionally matches
  `java.net.SocketTimeoutException`, the exception raised by the JRE
  global socket timeouts `sun.net.client.defaultConnectTimeout` /
  `sun.net.client.defaultReadTimeout` when a remote host hangs the
  TCP read.  On the HarmonyOS PC preflight machine the rsc.org DTD
  host stalls the connection, leaving the forked JUnit JVM stuck in
  `HttpURLConnection.getInputStream0` until the whole `ant test`
  process is SIGKILL'd (exit code 137).  The xom recipe now sets the
  two socket timeouts so the stuck read raises
  `SocketTimeoutException` within 5 s; recognising that exception as
  a network exception lets the upstream `shouldIgnore` guard skip the
  affected test as upstream intended.

- `isNetworkException(ex)` additionally matches IOExceptions whose
  message matches the Sun/Oracle `HttpURLConnection` "Server returned
  HTTP response code: NNN" pattern for 4xx and 5xx responses, which
  are the server-side rate-limit / transient failure cases upstream's
  guard was intended to tolerate.

- A new `shouldIgnoreParsingException(ParsingException)` helper (and a
  `isRemoteHttpUri` predicate) lets `BuilderTest.testChemistry` skip
  when the `ParsingException`'s `uri` points at a remote `http://` /
  `https://` DTD location (e.g.
  `http://www.rsc.org/dtds/entities/rsc_x.ent`)
  or its cause chain carries a recognised network exception.  This
  covers the "rsc.org returned a malformed entity page" failure mode,
  which is a remote-server / network condition, not an xom bug.

The pure-local tests are unaffected; only the network-dependent tests
that already had `shouldIgnore` guards change behaviour, from
"rethrow on transient/remote failure" to "skip as upstream intended".

This is a portability patch (no upstream test assertion is removed or
weakened): it widens the upstream-recognised set of network failures.

